[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the model I'd have argued for, and it means the task app needs a subscriptions table and a comment
  concept from the start.

  Next fork: [1mdelivery.[0m This one decides whether the system has external dependencies at all.

  In-app only (a bell icon and an inbox) needs nothing beyond the app itself — no third-party service, no configuration,
  no deliverability problems, and it's where the read/unread state has to live regardless. Email is the only channel
  that reaches someone who isn't currently looking at the app, which for an internal tool is most of the day; the cost
  is a real dependency (a sending service), plus unsubscribe handling and per-user preferences. Push notifications are
  the expensive one — service workers, permission prompts, per-browser quirks — and they buy little that email doesn't
  for a desk-bound team.

  What's cheap to change later: adding a channel, [3mif[0m the delivery layer is written channel-agnostically from the start.
  What's expensive: bolting a second channel onto code that assumed one, because preferences, batching, and dedup all
  have to be retrofitted at once.

  So my recommendation is in-app as the system of record plus email as a second channel, built behind a channel
  interface, and push explicitly out of scope until someone asks for it.
